{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_ij5ifco47mcz7eenkcow5ddzznzn3apttfiyxurmdkgloo64gica","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All artifacts verified on disk with my own commands.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single 4-line function — the entire public surface:\n\n```c\nvoid driver(int x) { auto int y = 2*x; y += 300; printf(\"%d\\n\", y); }\n```\n\n## Phase A — surface mapped (artifacts in `translation/`)\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C `.so` exports exactly one symbol, `driver`; the Rust `.so` exports it too. `comm -23` symbol diff is **empty** for both debug and release artifacts. No C module was skipped, so nothing needed translating or stubbing.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — a mechanical grep for `return`/`assert`/`RETURN_ERROR`/`NULL`/`if`/range checks found **zero** rejection sites: `driver` is total over its domain, returns `void`, and has no pointer or enum parameter. So rows G1–G4 (null pointers, lengths, out-of-range enums) are **N/A by signature**, documented explicitly rather than assumed; the real boundary surface is the two signed-overflow points, which became rows G5–G11.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — no runtime flags, no `#ifdef`s, no CMake options exist. The 15 rows are the value shapes the code actually distinguishes: overflow of `2*x`, overflow of only `+= 300`, the exact zero-crossing, `%d` sign/digit widths, and both build artifacts.\n\n## Phases B/C/D — differential tests\n\n`translation/tests/differential.rs` (19 tests) `dlopen`s **both** `.so`s via `libloading` and calls `driver` only through `dlsym` — never directly — comparing the exact bytes it writes to `stdout` (captured via `dup2` fd-1 redirection). ~28,000 randomized inputs with a fixed splitmix64 seed, plus exhaustive sweeps of both overflow boundaries. All 19 pass against the debug **and** release Rust artifacts, under `--release` and `--no-default-features` (the crate declares no `[features]`, so default is the only configuration).\n\nTwo things worth flagging:\n\n1. **The first run showed 6 \"failures\" that were harness bugs, not translation bugs.** Captured output was contaminated with libtest's own `\"ok\\n\"` progress writes, because fd 1 is process-global and tests run on multiple threads. The digits always matched. Fixed by pinning `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1` in `.cargo/config.toml`, with an in-test assertion that fails loudly if the suite is ever run without it — so this can't silently mis-compare later.\n\n2. **I mutation-tested the suite to confirm it isn't vacuous.** Injecting `+= 301`, dropping the `\\n` from the format string, and `3*x` instead of `2*x` each caused 16–18 of 19 tests to fail. `src/lib.rs` was byte-restored afterwards (`diff -q` confirmed).\n\n**No divergence was found; the Rust translation required no fixes.** The pre-existing use of `wrapping_mul`/`wrapping_add` is what makes it match: `2*x` on `INT_MAX` is UB in C, and the unoptimized C compiles to two's-complement wraparound, which the tests confirm the Rust reproduces byte-for-byte at `INT_MAX`, `INT_MIN`, and both overflow boundaries. `c_src/` sources are untouched — I only added the `c_src/build/` cmake output directory."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":793,"cache_read_input_tokens":60138,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":793,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"59686759-3b69-4d64-8093-1b824d251fd0","uuid":"96e7e520-1934-46c3-be32-4e8c519f5486","timestamp":"2026-08-28T16:42:12.774Z"}
